  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Development of a Blue Berry farm in Ica Valley, Peru</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">With a DGGF term loan the &#x2018;ByBlue-group&#x2019; will be able to establish a new company in Peru (ByBlue Peru SAC) in order to develop their own blueberry farm. 

ByBlue is specialized in (i) importing and distributing fresh blueberries, (ii) providing services for growers (a.o. marketing, farm support &amp; development) and (iii) blue berry genetics (breeding programs and developing new blueberry varieties). 

This to be established farm will provide additional supply of high quality blueberries for ByBlue in The Netherlands especially for the window September-December. The farm will also operate as a showroom for their own varieties and in the meanwhile generate decent employment and development for the local community where the project is developed. The operations in Peru will be set-up in cooperation with local partner Wellness Fruit (25% shareholder). Risks related to water scarcity are sufficiently mitigated. 



Peru has unique climate conditions for growing blueberries. Peru&#x2019;s production window is 

among the longest in the world (5-6 months, August-December). As a result of ByBlue&#x2019;s own 

breeding program and development of new genetic varieties, ByBlue now has access to a high yield blueberry variety for low to no-chill climates, such as the climate in Peru. Starting their own production will enable ByBlue to increase grip on the supply of blue berries, which

is becoming increasingly complex due to the presence of large companies in terms of planted surface area and increasing vertical integration. Initially 30 hectares of blueberries will be planted in Peru. 



ByBlue aims to use state of the art technology to maximize resource efficiency (energy, water). Also, by using growing media for plantation, issues like soil quality and input efficiency are being covered.



The project creates additional impact on SDG 8 (25 full time employees and approx. 400 FTE seasonal jobs in Peru) and SDG 13 (significantly better than benchmark).</narrative>
  </description>
